            <ArticleTitle>Sodium hypochlorite accident with evaluation by cone beam computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>492-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1365-2591.2011.02009.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To show the radiographic manifestation of sodium hypochlorite after accidental injection past the apical foramen and into the soft tissues.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A female patient was seen for an emergency visit after suffering a sodium hypochlorite accident at her general dentist's office. The patient was seen within 1 h of the accident and was in pain associated with facial swelling. Radiographs, including a Cone Beam Computed Tomography (CBCT), and photographs were taken. Endodontic emergency treatment was initiated. The patient was reassured and given pain medication and antibiotics. Follow-up visits were scheduled over 6 days when the swelling had resolved.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="KEY LEARNING POINTS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">• Importance of multiple radiographic images during preoperative endodontic evaluation when undertaking endodontic retreatment. • Knowledge of apical anatomy as related to surrounding structures. • Effect of sodium hypochlorite when injected in the soft tissues.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2012 International Endodontic Journal.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
